1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,080
This is Clark, and I want to take a moment to thank the hundreds of scouters all over the world who sponsor the podcast by being ScoutmasterCG.com backers and patrons.

2
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:17,700
You can join them by going to ScoutmasterCG.com and following the support or be a patron links at the top of the page.

3
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,120
And now it's to you, Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:38,040
It occurs to me, for generations, men have looked upon the bald eagle and found great inspiration, yet when the eagle looks upon a bald man, it doesn't seem to affect him at all.

5
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,400
Hey, this is podcast number one.

6
00:00:50,120 --> 00:01:13,000
So there it is, the Happy Wanderer.

7
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,820
I think it makes like a kind of a fun theme song for the podcast, you know?

8
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:22,300
I mean, we sing it in the dining hall and around the campfire every once in a while.

9
00:01:22,639 --> 00:01:24,559
It's a great scout song.

10
00:01:25,059 --> 00:01:27,500
So what is all this scouting stuff about?

11
00:01:27,679 --> 00:01:29,280
Why are we doing a podcast?

12
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:40,440
We're going to attempt to answer these questions and more, but just by way of introduction, I am a Scoutmaster and have been a Scoutmaster for 25 years this year.

13
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:41,699
Yes, thank you very much.

14
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:45,660
Why have I spent this much time dealing with it?

15
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:57,940
Well, I'll tell you what, you can go anywhere where there are Scoutage boys, a classroom, a village square, anywhere in the world, and you will make some observations and conclusions.

16
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,300
Boys instinctively form groups.

17
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:02,780
They adopt some kind of uniform.

18
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,240
They establish standards for their group.

19
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:10,240
They develop a credo and they create initiatory challenges.

20
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:10,979
What?

21
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,160
Initiatory what?

22
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:14,880
Okay, they dare each other to do stuff.

23
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:16,859
Does that make sense, doesn't it?

24
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:17,720
I was a boy.

25
00:02:18,079 --> 00:02:19,720
These were things that were common with us.

26
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:27,960
We formed little gangs and groups and clubs and they weren't necessarily the official adult run ones and stuff like that.

27
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:31,019
But these were just instinctive things that we did.

28
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:36,960
I mean, we yearned to belong and to gain acceptance and approval from outside the confines of our family.

29
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:37,600
Right?

30
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:37,700
Right?

31
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:43,800
And this kind of search can be kind of imperfect because, hey, you're a kid.

32
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:58,820
A lot of times when young people, especially in their adolescence, are looking and searching and trying things on and we kind of look askance at that and we regard it with some real misapprehension.

33
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:04,200
I mean, in adolescence, we try on lots of attitudes and poses and stuff.

34
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,820
You know what I said about uniforms there, about groups of boys adopting uniforms?

35
00:03:08,959 --> 00:03:09,420
Well, they do.

36
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:11,820
They don't call it a uniform.

37
00:03:12,019 --> 00:03:22,180
It might be like a black t-shirt and black jeans and a dog collar or some kind of punky kind of thing.

38
00:03:22,359 --> 00:03:24,959
I'm woefully outdated, okay?

39
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:27,280
But you understand what I mean.

40
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:34,780
You tried to drive your parents crazy by wearing certain kinds of clothing and adopting certain kinds of attitude and wearing your hair a certain way.

41
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:35,540
Right?

42
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:36,220
I did.

43
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:39,900
And it's just the right of every successive generation.

44
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:42,500
What does scouting have to do with any of this?

45
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:52,980
Well, in most systems of education, and scouting, I think, can very broadly define to fit into the world of systems of education.

46
00:03:52,980 --> 00:04:03,760
In most systems of education, you take these instinctive things, forming groups and uniforms and initiatory challenges and things like that, and you don't capitalize on them.

47
00:04:03,839 --> 00:04:07,140
As a matter of fact, you outlaw them and you resist them.

48
00:04:07,339 --> 00:04:09,640
I mean, the school system does a pretty good job of that.

49
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:15,339
But scouting capitalizes on these instinctive things and turns them into good things.

50
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:21,060
We all, more or less, hammered our way through adolescence, you know, blindly.

51
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:25,440
We may have had people who made things a little easier for us.

52
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:29,600
Friends and mentors, you know, coaches, teachers, scout leaders.

53
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:33,920
And there were probably people that made things a little more confusing and difficult for us.

54
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:40,620
The reason that I am a scoutmaster is because I want to be one of the people that makes this process a little easier.

55
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:47,100
Plus, I love to go camping and cook over a campfire and all that type of stuff.

56
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:50,240
Scouting, I have found, it's not an ideology.

57
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:55,160
It has a credo and, you know, we have the Scout Oath and Law and things like that.

58
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:04,740
But it's more a way of taking and channeling the unstable energies and excesses of adolescence into something positive

59
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:09,640
and something that will help to shape rather than destroy a person's life.

60
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:16,160
I've got to tell you that after 25 years of looking around and experiencing this, it doesn't always work.

61
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:17,480
No, it really doesn't.

62
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:24,200
And when it doesn't work, I can tell you that it's usually not the fault of the boys that are involved.

63
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:31,460
It's the fault of the adults administering the program who usually innocently just don't understand what they're doing

64
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:35,719
or get off on the wrong track and then they get frustrated because things don't work.

65
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:36,980
Well, I'll tell you something.

66
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:40,159
That is why I am doing the podcast.

67
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:45,099
Just kind of as a helping hand, I'm not smarter than you.

68
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:47,740
I just have been around a little bit longer.

69
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:52,900
And hopefully I can help you avoid some of the pitfalls that I have had as a scoutmaster.

70
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:58,299
And enjoy some of the benefits that I've been able to enjoy.

71
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:00,359
It's been a great ride.

72
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:05,140
I've gotten to do things and go places that I would otherwise not have done.

73
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:10,120
I've gotten to work with lots and lots of wonderful people and wonderful scouts.

74
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:15,760
And I am really eager to further the journey with this podcast.

75
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:18,340
And I invite you to come on along.

76
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:48,159
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

77
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:54,560
I served as the camp director for a couple of years at our camp.

78
00:06:54,599 --> 00:06:55,840
And I was on staff for many years.

79
00:06:55,900 --> 00:07:00,820
One of the old timers there at camp told me a story about when he was a camp director.

80
00:07:01,679 --> 00:07:08,780
And a scoutmaster was in camp who was later to become a state senator and who shall remain nameless.

81
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:14,700
But he told my friend to come on down to the campsite that evening because the troop was going to have a campfire.

82
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:18,400
And he was going to show the boys the fire snake.

83
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:28,919
So my buddy was interested enough and he went down to the campfire.

84
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:35,900
And you know what happened is this guy had taken a can of black powder.

85
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:46,099
Now, if you're a shooter or deal with firearms at all, you know the difference between black powder and smokeless powder.

86
00:07:46,659 --> 00:07:46,780
Okay.

87
00:07:47,260 --> 00:07:50,760
So black powder is really explosive.

88
00:07:51,159 --> 00:07:55,960
I mean, if you touch a match to a pile of black powder, it goes up like a camera flash.

89
00:07:56,140 --> 00:07:56,419
Poof.

90
00:07:56,420 --> 00:08:01,020
If you touch a match to a pile of smokeless powder, it burns a little slower.

91
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:04,460
That's why it always kind of drives you crazy when you see an old western movie.

92
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:08,680
Somebody takes a powder keg and they trail a line of powder along the ground.

93
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:13,160
And they put the powder keg under the wagon.

94
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:14,960
They want to blow up or something like that.

95
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:17,460
And so they use that line of powder like a fuse.

96
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:19,460
And then they light it.

97
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:24,100
And in the movies, it goes very slowly along the line as the tension builds.

98
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,220
And finally, you know, they blow up whatever they're blowing up.

99
00:08:27,580 --> 00:08:29,240
Well, it wouldn't work that way with black powder.

100
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:34,960
You see, as soon as you touch a match to one end, in a heartbeat, that entire line would just go poof.

101
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:40,800
All right.

102
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:42,420
So let's get back to the campfire, right?

103
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:44,220
That's why I told you about it.

104
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:52,400
Because the fire snake was a little trench that the Scoutmaster had dug in a spiral around the outside of the fire, right?

105
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:54,500
And it had filled with black powder.

106
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:56,340
Black powder.

107
00:08:57,060 --> 00:08:57,959
Not smokeless powder.

108
00:08:58,420 --> 00:08:59,920
But old-fashioned black powder.

109
00:09:00,959 --> 00:09:04,180
And then he very carefully camouflaged it and everything like that.

110
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:06,660
And he brought the boys down into the campfire circle.

111
00:09:07,420 --> 00:09:12,260
And you can tell how old this story is because the Scoutmaster was smoking a big cigar.

112
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:15,600
Can't do that anymore, fellas.

113
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:17,100
And a good thing, too.

114
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:22,460
So he's smoking a big cigar and he proceeds to tell the guys this big tale about the fire snake.

115
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:27,000
And, you know, weaves a big imaginary tale and everything like that.

116
00:09:27,220 --> 00:09:32,340
And he bends down to touch his cigar to the end of the fire snake.

117
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:38,160
And he thinks what's going to happen is what happens in the movies where it just do-do-do-do-do just kind of burns around in a fuse.

118
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:43,980
And boys would just be excited and delighted with this fantastic effect that would end in the ignition of their fire.

119
00:09:43,980 --> 00:10:04,140
And, you know, what it did, actually, was all that powder in the little trough that he had made around the fire blew up at once, just in a heartbeat.

120
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:09,920
And my buddy was there to watch and he said there was a big bright flash.

121
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:14,939
And, you know, 15 or 20 scouts knocked off their benches.

122
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:25,800
And their faces blackened by the...

123
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:27,820
He saw nobody was hurt.

124
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:30,380
He said he just turned around and headed back up the trail.

125
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:34,340
He never really talked with that scoutmaster again about that particular incident.

126
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:56,420
Over the next several podcasts, we're going to be talking about instructional methods for scouts.

127
00:10:56,720 --> 00:11:00,420
This is going to serve as an introduction this time.

128
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:08,380
But what you're going to be able to do is to find all this information written down on the blog as well.

129
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:17,719
There's a PDF file of all of the information that you can use as an outline to remind you or to use in training or whatever.

130
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:25,620
We need to put all of our instruction in the context of what we're trying to accomplish as scouts.

131
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:28,020
For this, I turn to Baden-Powell.

132
00:11:28,540 --> 00:11:33,400
And I'm going to recommend to you Baden-Powell's Aids to Scout Mastership.

133
00:11:33,460 --> 00:11:37,700
It's a 70-page booklet that's widely available online as a PDF file.

134
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:39,200
It's well worth reading.

135
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:47,300
And I think it's a great touchstone to return to, especially when you're looking at very broad concepts such as we are right now.

136
00:11:47,400 --> 00:11:50,500
We're instructing scouts in skills or information.

137
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:53,620
And how are we going to evaluate the quality of our instruction?

138
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:56,320
Because that informs the way we instruct, right?

139
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:58,900
So, this is what Baden-Powell had to say.

140
00:11:59,060 --> 00:11:59,400
He says,

141
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:08,060
When it is applied with understanding and consideration, the advancement program fosters encouragement and ambition regardless of an individual boy's abilities.

142
00:12:08,440 --> 00:12:14,500
It's for this reason that the standard of proficiency is purposefully left undefined.

143
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:25,660
Our standard is not the attainment of a certain level of quality of knowledge or skill, but the amount of effort the boy has put into acquiring such knowledge or skill.

144
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:30,120
This brings the most inept onto equal footing with his more capable brother.

145
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:33,720
Evaluation for badges is not competitive.

146
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:41,440
The Scoutmaster should judge each individual case on its merits and discriminating where to be generous and where to tighten up.

147
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:45,760
Some are inclined to insist that all scouts should be expert before they can get a badge.

148
00:12:45,860 --> 00:12:47,440
This is very right in theory.

149
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:53,440
You can get a few boys pretty proficient in this way, but our object is to get all the boys interested.

150
00:12:54,180 --> 00:13:07,760
The Scoutmaster who rewards effort, the Scoutmaster who rewards effort as opposed to expertise, develops confidence and enthusiasm, whereas a demanding standard of performance makes boys reluctant and hesitant.

151
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:08,760
Okay?

152
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:11,460
So, that's what Vaden Powell had to say about this.

153
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:16,660
He goes on to address what you're thinking in your mind right now, probably.

154
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:17,260
He says,

155
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:21,620
The other extreme is almost giving away badges on a very slight knowledge of the subjects.

156
00:13:21,819 --> 00:13:26,660
Scoutmasters should use their sense and discretion, keeping the main aim in view.

157
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:32,500
There is always the danger of badge hunting supplanting badge earning.

158
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:40,100
Our main aim is to encourage initiative and self-confidence instead of showy self-indulgence.

159
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:49,700
The Scoutmaster must be alert to check badge hunting and to realize which is the badge hunter and which is the eager and earnest worker.

160
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:57,660
The success of the advancement program depends very largely on the Scoutmaster himself and his individual handling of it.

161
00:13:57,740 --> 00:14:01,220
Now, that informs the way that we instruct.

162
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:01,780
Okay?

163
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:15,420
I mean, Scout-age boys are inquisitive and they're investigative and they're eager to learn and their natural curiosity is right there just below the surface and sometimes not below the surface at all.

164
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:23,240
Conversely, they can be really unresponsive and lifeless when they're compelled rather than inspired to learn.

165
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:26,180
We're not trying to make them into experts on anything.

166
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:36,420
We're trying to inspire what Albert Einstein called the holy curiosity of inquiry by exciting their curiosity and their interest.

167
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:37,880
Baden-Powell said,

168
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:47,459
If we make scouting into a formal scheme of serious instruction and efficiency, we miss the whole point in value of scout training.

169
00:14:47,859 --> 00:14:53,520
Infective methods for instructing scouts aren't just classroom methods from school.

170
00:14:53,520 --> 00:15:00,000
As a matter of fact, you want to eliminate as many classroom school-based methods as you can.

171
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:04,680
And you don't want to just disguise them with some semantic trick of renaming them.

172
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:06,360
They're actually different.

173
00:15:06,579 --> 00:15:08,160
Let's have a few ground rules.

174
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:12,060
First, there are no lectures lasting more than two minutes.

175
00:15:12,439 --> 00:15:18,980
And for our purposes, we're going to define a lecture as scouts sitting and listening while somebody talks.

176
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:23,800
That's a tough one, especially if, like me, you like the sound of your own voice.

177
00:15:24,120 --> 00:15:28,060
Second, no pre-printed written tests or evaluations.

178
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:28,860
None.

179
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:30,380
No worksheets.

180
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:31,600
Nothing like that.

181
00:15:31,820 --> 00:15:33,080
I've used them in the past.

182
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:33,920
Get rid of them.

183
00:15:34,120 --> 00:15:34,480
Shred them.

184
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:35,180
Tear them up.

185
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:36,720
Informational handouts.

186
00:15:37,020 --> 00:15:39,700
They're okay, but you're only allowed one page.

187
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,340
No big giant stapled bundle of handouts.

188
00:15:43,660 --> 00:15:44,700
No note-taking.

189
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,120
Maybe a couple of little things here and there.

190
00:15:47,380 --> 00:15:47,980
No homework.

191
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:48,580
No homework.

192
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:50,100
Oh my God, no homework.

193
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:54,740
We've eliminated most of the methods that most people use instructing scouts.

194
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:56,180
We've gotten rid of lectures.

195
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:59,340
We've gotten rid of written tests and evaluations.

196
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:03,100
We're saying, you know, your handout can only be one piece of paper.

197
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:06,240
No note-taking and, God forbid, homework.

198
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:06,960
No homework.

199
00:16:07,180 --> 00:16:07,960
So what are we going to do?

200
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:10,640
You're going to have to follow up on the next several podcasts.

201
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:18,100
And we will talk about the different methods that we can apply to scout instruction to make it what it ought to be.

202
